    "description": "<p>We answer the historical distributed dense-coding question for the symmetric four-qubit W state in the local-unitary model of Bruss and collaborators. Two independent senders transmit one qubit each to different receivers. A complete Bell instrument at one receiver retains a classical-quantum output at the other; the established cq multiple-access coding theorem gives an achievable sum-rate supremum at least 3/2 + h2(1/4) = 2.311278124459... bits per resource copy. Both senders can achieve 9/8 bits per copy with vanishing uniform average error, establishing the original-model LOCC-DC classification.</p><p>The Bell decomposition, Bell forwarding, cqMAC coding theorem and prior general MAC dense-coding framework are explicitly credited. The bounded primary-literature audit found no earlier explicit or established-equivalent achievement of the exact target in inspected sources; specific access and version limitations are documented. No categorical firstness or worldwide continued openness, exact optimal capacity, one-copy advantage, zero-error or maximal-error claim is made.</p><p>AI tools were used extensively in solving, drafting, reproducing and adversarially reviewing the result. This is an unrefereed preprint without conventional human peer review. The package includes the manuscript PDF and standalone LaTeX source, a standard-library exact rational verifier, recorded results, a priority-audit supplement, license and file digests. Finite computations supplement the analytical proof and established asymptotic coding theorem.</p>"
  },
